  <p>Pick a mode, enter two values, and read the formula-based result.</p>
  <h3>Mode 1: Find Scale Factor</h3>
  <p>Enter the original side length and the matching similar side length. The calculator divides the similar side by the original side and reports <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} />, the scale factor from the original triangle to the similar one.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[k=\\frac{\\text{Similar Side}}{\\text{Original Side}}]]]')} /></p>
  <h3>Mode 2: Find Missing Similar Side</h3>
  <p>Enter the original side length and the scale factor <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} />. The calculator multiplies the original side by <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} /> and reports the matching side of the similar triangle.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{\\text{Similar Side}=\\text{Original Side}\\times k}]]]')} /></p>
  <h3>Mode 3: Find Original Side</h3>
  <p>Enter the similar side length and the scale factor <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} />. The calculator divides the similar side by <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} /> and reports the matching side of the original triangle. Division reverses the scaling, so this mode works backward from an enlarged or reduced measurement.</p>
  <p>Choose the mode that matches your known values, then check that the two sides you enter actually correspond: shorter leg with shorter leg, longer leg with longer leg, hypotenuse with hypotenuse. Enter positive numbers in the same unit, click Calculate, and read the result with its step-by-step working. Click Reset to clear the fields and start a new calculation.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 3: sec-what -->
<section id="sec-what" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Are Similar Right Triangles?</h2>
  <p>Similar right triangles share a shape but not necessarily a size.</p>
  <p>Two right triangles are similar when their corresponding angles are equal and their corresponding side lengths are proportional. Both contain a 90° angle, and their acute angles match in pairs, so the triangles have exactly the same shape. Their sizes may differ: one can be twice as large, half as large, or any positive multiple of the other.</p>
  <p>Take a 3-4-5 triangle and a 6-8-10 triangle. The shorter legs are <span set:html={rich('[[3]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[6]]')} />, the longer legs are <span set:html={rich('[[4]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[8]]')} />, and the hypotenuses are <span set:html={rich('[[5]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[10]]')} />. Every ratio equals <span set:html={rich('[[2]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\frac{6}{3}=\\frac{8}{4}=\\frac{10}{5}=2]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Because all three ratios agree, the triangles are similar with scale factor <span set:html={rich('[[k=2]]')} />. If you need a missing side of a single right triangle rather than a comparison between two, use the <a href="/right-triangle-side-calculator/">right triangle side calculator</a> instead.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('similar-triangles-two.svg')} alt="Two similar right triangles, 3-4-5 and 6-8-10, with matching right angles and corresponding sides labelled" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">The 3-4-5 and 6-8-10 triangles are similar with scale factor 2.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 4: sec-similar-rule -->
<section id="sec-similar-rule" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Makes Two Right Triangles Similar?</h2>
  <p>One matching acute angle is enough to prove similarity between right triangles.</p>
  <p>The Angle-Angle (AA) rule says two triangles are similar when two pairs of corresponding angles are equal. For right triangles, the first pair is free: both contain a 90° angle. That leaves only one acute angle to check. If one acute angle matches, the third angles must also match, because the angles of any triangle add to 180°.</p>
  <p>Compare a 30°-60°-90° triangle with another 30°-60°-90° triangle of a different size. The right angles match, the 30° angles match, and the remaining angles are both 60°, since <span set:html={rich('[[180^\\circ-90^\\circ-30^\\circ=60^\\circ]]')} />. The AA rule then guarantees similarity, whatever the side lengths are.</p>
  <p>Equal angles force proportional sides, so the scale factor is the same for every corresponding pair. The rule works at any size: a tiny 30°-60°-90° triangle and a large one are similar for exactly the same reason.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('similar-triangles-aa.svg')} alt="Two right triangles with 90 degree angles and matching 30 degree acute angles, showing AA similarity" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Both triangles have 90° and 30° angles, so AA makes them similar.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 5: sec-corresponding -->
<section id="sec-corresponding" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding Corresponding Sides in Similar Right Triangles</h2>
  <p>Corresponding sides sit in matching positions relative to equal angles.</p>
  <p>Corresponding sides are the sides that occupy the same position in each triangle, measured against equal angles. The shorter leg of one triangle corresponds to the shorter leg of the other, the longer leg matches the longer leg, and the hypotenuse matches the hypotenuse. In triangle ABC and similar triangle DEF, the correspondence reads <span set:html={rich('[[AB \\leftrightarrow DE]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[BC \\leftrightarrow EF]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[AC \\leftrightarrow DF]]')} /> when the vertices are labelled in matching order.</p>
  <p>Matching the wrong sides is the most common error in similarity problems. Dividing a hypotenuse by a leg mixes two different positions, so the ratio means nothing and the answer is wrong. Always identify the correspondence first: compare angles, label the matching vertices, and only then divide.</p>
  <p>A reliable check is size order: the longest side of one triangle always corresponds to the longest side of the other, and the shortest to the shortest. When the side ratios all agree, the matching is correct.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('similar-triangles-corresponding.svg')} alt="Triangles ABC and DEF with matching colors marking corresponding sides AB with DE, BC with EF, and AC with DF" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Matching colors mark corresponding sides in triangles ABC and DEF.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 6: sec-scale-factor -->
<section id="sec-scale-factor" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Is the Scale Factor in Similar Triangles?</h2>
  <p>The scale factor k says how much each side grows or shrinks.</p>
  <p>The scale factor <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} /> is the ratio of a similar side to its matching original side. It describes the change from the original triangle to the similar triangle, and it applies to every corresponding pair at once.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[k=\\frac{\\text{Similar Side}}{\\text{Original Side}}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>When <span set:html={rich('[[k=1]]')} />, the triangles are the same size and also congruent. When <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} /> is greater than <span set:html={rich('[[1]]')} />, the similar triangle is larger: <span set:html={rich('[[k=2]]')} /> doubles every side and <span set:html={rich('[[k=3]]')} /> triples every side. When <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} /> lies between <span set:html={rich('[[0]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[1]]')} />, the similar triangle is smaller: <span set:html={rich('[[k=0.5]]')} /> halves every side and <span set:html={rich('[[k=0.25]]')} /> quarters it. The scale factor must always be positive, because a zero factor would collapse the triangle and a negative factor has no meaning as a length change.</p>
  <p>Scaling never changes the angles. A triangle enlarged by <span set:html={rich('[[k=3]]')} /> keeps the same acute angles, so its shape is unchanged; only the measurements grow. The table below summarises the main cases.</p>
  <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-6">
    <table class="w-full text-sm my-6 border-collapse">
      <thead>
        <tr><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Scale factor</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Effect on side lengths</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2"><span set:html={rich('[[k=1]]')} /></td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Same size, congruent</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2"><span set:html={rich('[[k>1]]')} /></td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Larger triangle</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2"><span set:html={rich('[[0<k<1]]')} /></td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Smaller triangle</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2"><span set:html={rich('[[k=2]]')} /></td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Every side doubles</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2"><span set:html={rich('[[k=0.5]]')} /></td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Every side halves</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Section 7: sec-formula-k -->
<section id="sec-formula-k" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Formula to Find the Scale Factor</h2>
  <p>Divide the similar side by the original side to find k.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{k=\\frac{\\text{Similar Side}}{\\text{Original Side}}}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Take an original side of <span set:html={rich('[[5]]')} /> and a matching similar side of <span set:html={rich('[[15]]')} />. The scale factor is <span set:html={rich('[[k=15/5=3]]')} />, so the similar triangle is three times as large in linear dimensions. The same calculation works with any corresponding pair: the hypotenuses give the same <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} /> as the legs, provided the sides truly correspond.</p>
  <p>The direction matters. Dividing the original side by the similar side gives the reciprocal, <span set:html={rich('[[1/k]]')} />, which describes scaling in the opposite direction. For the calculator, always put the similar side on top and the original side underneath.</p>
  <p>Use Mode 1 of the calculator for this job: enter the two matching lengths, click Calculate, and read <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} /> with the division shown step by step.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('similar-triangles-scale-factor.svg')} alt="Original side 6 and similar side 18 with an arrow showing k equals 18 divided by 6 equals 3" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Original side 6 and similar side 18 give k = 18 / 6 = 3.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 8: sec-formula-similar -->
<section id="sec-formula-similar" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Formula to Find a Missing Similar Side</h2>
  <p>Multiply the original side by k to find the matching similar side.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{\\text{Similar Side}=\\text{Original Side}\\times k}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Take an original side of <span set:html={rich('[[7]]')} /> and a scale factor of <span set:html={rich('[[k=2.5]]')} />. The matching similar side is <span set:html={rich('[[7\\times2.5=17.5]]')} />. Multiplication moves forward from the original triangle to the scaled triangle, so the result is the corresponding side in the enlarged figure.</p>
  <p>The same formula handles reductions. With an original side of <span set:html={rich('[[10]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[k=0.4]]')} />, the similar side is <span set:html={rich('[[10\\times0.4=4]]')} />. A scale factor below <span set:html={rich('[[1]]')} /> still uses multiplication; only the result is smaller than the starting side.</p>
  <p>Check the answer by dividing it back: <span set:html={rich('[[17.5/7=2.5]]')} /> returns the scale factor, which confirms the multiplication was set up correctly. Use Mode 2 of the calculator for this job, entering the original side and <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} /> in the same unit.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('similar-triangles-missing-side.svg')} alt="Original side 8 multiplied by scale factor 2.5 giving the missing similar side 20" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Original side 8 times k = 2.5 gives the similar side 20.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 9: sec-formula-original -->
<section id="sec-formula-original" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Formula to Find the Original Side</h2>
  <p>Divide the similar side by k to recover the original side.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{\\text{Original Side}=\\frac{\\text{Similar Side}}{k}}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Take a similar side of <span set:html={rich('[[24]]')} /> and a scale factor of <span set:html={rich('[[k=3]]')} />. The original side is <span set:html={rich('[[24/3=8]]')} />. Division reverses multiplication: the similar side was made by multiplying the original side by <span set:html={rich('[[3]]')} />, so dividing by <span set:html={rich('[[3]]')} /> undoes the scaling.</p>
  <p>Decimal scale factors work the same way. With a similar side of <span set:html={rich('[[21]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[k=1.5]]')} />, the original side is <span set:html={rich('[[21/1.5=14]]')} />. Keep the units consistent and check the result by multiplying back: <span set:html={rich('[[14\\times1.5=21]]')} />.</p>
  <p>Use Mode 3 of the calculator for this job, entering the similar side and <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} />. This mode is the one to reach for when a scaled drawing or model gives you a measurement and you need the real size behind it.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('similar-triangles-original-side.svg')} alt="Similar side 15 divided by scale factor 3 giving the original side 5, with a reverse arrow" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Similar side 15 divided by k = 3 gives the original side 5.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 10: sec-enlarge -->
<section id="sec-enlarge" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding Enlargement and Reduction</h2>
  <p>A scale factor above 1 enlarges; below 1 it reduces.</p>
  <p>Enlargement and reduction are the same operation in opposite directions. Multiplying every side by <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} /> changes the size of the triangle while the angles stay fixed, so the shape is preserved exactly. The direction of the comparison decides whether <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} /> is greater or less than <span set:html={rich('[[1]]')} />: going from a small triangle to a large one gives <span set:html={rich('[[k>1]]')} />, while going from a large triangle to a small one gives <span set:html={rich('[[k<1]]')} />.</p>
  <p>A scale factor of <span set:html={rich('[[1]]')} /> leaves the triangle unchanged, so the two triangles are congruent as well as similar. At <span set:html={rich('[[k=0.5]]')} /> every side halves, and at <span set:html={rich('[[k=2]]')} /> every side doubles, yet the acute angles are identical in all three versions. That is why a scale drawing and the real object it represents can look exactly alike at very different sizes.</p>
  <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-6">
    <table class="w-full text-sm my-6 border-collapse">
      <thead>
        <tr><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Scale factor</th><th class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Effect</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2"><span set:html={rich('[[0.25]]')} /></td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">One-quarter size</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2"><span set:html={rich('[[0.5]]')} /></td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Half size</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2"><span set:html={rich('[[0.75]]')} /></td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Three-quarters size</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2"><span set:html={rich('[[1]]')} /></td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Same size</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2"><span set:html={rich('[[1.5]]')} /></td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">1.5 times larger</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2"><span set:html={rich('[[2]]')} /></td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Twice as large</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2"><span set:html={rich('[[3]]')} /></td><td class="border-gray-600 px-4 py-2">Three times larger</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('similar-triangles-enlarge-reduce.svg')} alt="Three similar right triangles with scale factors 0.5, 1, and 2, showing reduction, same size, and enlargement" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Scale factors 0.5, 1, and 2 give a smaller, equal, and larger triangle.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 11: sec-proportions -->
<section id="sec-proportions" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Set Up Proportions Between Similar Right Triangles</h2>
  <p>One proportion links every pair of corresponding sides.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{\\frac{a\'}{a}=\\frac{b\'}{b}=\\frac{c\'}{c}=k}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Here <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> are the sides of the original triangle and <span set:html={rich('[[a\']]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b\']]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[c\']]')} /> are the matching sides of the similar triangle. The proportion says all three ratios equal the same scale factor <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} />. If any ratio differs from the others, either the sides are mismatched or the triangles are not similar.</p>
  <p>Check the 3-4-5 and 6-8-10 triangles: <span set:html={rich('[[6/3=2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[8/4=2]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[10/5=2]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[k=2]]')} /> and the proportion holds. A proportion can also solve for a missing side. Suppose <span set:html={rich('[[x/6=15/10]]')} />: the right side equals <span set:html={rich('[[1.5]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[x=6\\times1.5=9]]')} />. Cross-multiplication gives the same result and matches the calculator's missing similar side mode, which multiplies the original side by <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} />.</p>
  <p>Proportions also let you test a claim of similarity without the calculator: compute the three ratios by hand, and agreement across all three is the evidence you need.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 12: sec-check-similar -->
<section id="sec-check-similar" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Check Whether Two Right Triangles Are Similar</h2>
  <p>Six checks confirm whether two right triangles are similar.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Confirm both triangles are right triangles.</li>
    <li>Identify the acute angles of each triangle.</li>
    <li>Check whether one corresponding acute angle is equal.</li>
    <li>Match the corresponding sides: shorter leg to shorter leg, longer leg to longer leg, hypotenuse to hypotenuse.</li>
    <li>Compare the ratios of the corresponding side lengths.</li>
    <li>Confirm the scale factor is the same for every pair.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Test the 3-4-5 and 6-8-10 triangles. Both are right triangles, and their acute angles match, so the AA rule already establishes similarity. The side ratios confirm it: <span set:html={rich('[[6/3=2]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[8/4=2]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[10/5=2]]')} />. All three ratios agree, so <span set:html={rich('[[k=2]]')} /> is consistent.</p>
  <p>Equal angles and consistent ratios are two routes to the same conclusion. Angles prove similarity through AA, while equal ratios verify it numerically. If either test fails, the triangles are not similar and no scale factor applies.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 13: sec-worked -->
<section id="sec-worked" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Worked Examples Using the Similar Right Triangles Calculator</h2>
  <p>Eight examples across all three modes, with the working shown.</p>
  <h3>Example 1: Find the Scale Factor</h3>
  <p>Problem: an original side is <span set:html={rich('[[6]]')} /> and the matching similar side is <span set:html={rich('[[18]]')} />. Mode: Find Scale Factor. Formula: <span set:html={rich('[[k=\\text{Similar Side}/\\text{Original Side}]]')} />. Substitution: <span set:html={rich('[[k=18/6=3]]')} />. Answer: <span set:html={rich('[[k=3]]')} />. The similar triangle is three times as large in every linear dimension, with the same angles.</p>
  <h3>Example 2: Find the Scale Factor From a Different Pair</h3>
  <p>Problem: an original side is <span set:html={rich('[[4]]')} /> and the matching similar side is <span set:html={rich('[[10]]')} />. Mode: Find Scale Factor. Formula: <span set:html={rich('[[k=\\text{Similar Side}/\\text{Original Side}]]')} />. Substitution: <span set:html={rich('[[k=10/4=2.5]]')} />. Answer: <span set:html={rich('[[k=2.5]]')} />. The similar triangle is two and a half times as large as the original.</p>
  <h3>Example 3: Find a Missing Similar Side</h3>
  <p>Problem: an original side is <span set:html={rich('[[8]]')} /> and the scale factor is <span set:html={rich('[[k=2.5]]')} />. Mode: Find Missing Similar Side. Formula: <span set:html={rich('[[\\text{Similar Side}=\\text{Original Side}\\times k]]')} />. Substitution: <span set:html={rich('[[8\\times2.5=20]]')} />. Answer: <span set:html={rich('[[20]]')} />. The matching side of the similar triangle is 20 units long.</p>
  <h3>Example 4: Find a Reduced Similar Side</h3>
  <p>Problem: an original side is <span set:html={rich('[[12]]')} /> and the scale factor is <span set:html={rich('[[k=0.5]]')} />. Mode: Find Missing Similar Side. Formula: <span set:html={rich('[[\\text{Similar Side}=\\text{Original Side}\\times k]]')} />. Substitution: <span set:html={rich('[[12\\times0.5=6]]')} />. Answer: <span set:html={rich('[[6]]')} />. A scale factor below <span set:html={rich('[[1]]')} /> reduces the triangle, so the similar side is half the original.</p>
  <h3>Example 5: Find an Original Side</h3>
  <p>Problem: a similar side is <span set:html={rich('[[15]]')} /> and the scale factor is <span set:html={rich('[[k=3]]')} />. Mode: Find Original Side. Formula: <span set:html={rich('[[\\text{Original Side}=\\text{Similar Side}/k]]')} />. Substitution: <span set:html={rich('[[15/3=5]]')} />. Answer: <span set:html={rich('[[5]]')} />. The matching side of the original triangle is 5 units long.</p>
  <h3>Example 6: Find an Original Side With a Decimal Scale Factor</h3>
  <p>Problem: a similar side is <span set:html={rich('[[18]]')} /> and the scale factor is <span set:html={rich('[[k=1.5]]')} />. Mode: Find Original Side. Formula: <span set:html={rich('[[\\text{Original Side}=\\text{Similar Side}/k]]')} />. Substitution: <span set:html={rich('[[18/1.5=12]]')} />. Answer: <span set:html={rich('[[12]]')} />. Check by multiplying back: <span set:html={rich('[[12\\times1.5=18]]')} />.</p>
  <h3>Example 7: Compare Complete Similar Triangles</h3>
  <p>Problem: compare a 3-4-5 triangle with a scale factor of <span set:html={rich('[[k=2]]')} />. Mode: Find Missing Similar Side, applied to each side in turn. The similar sides are <span set:html={rich('[[3\\times2=6]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[4\\times2=8]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[5\\times2=10]]')} />. The ratios check out: <span set:html={rich('[[6/3=8/4=10/5=2]]')} />. Answer: the similar triangle is 6-8-10. Note the calculator takes one side at a time; it does not accept a whole triangle in a single calculation.</p>
