</section>

<!-- Section 14: sec-pythagoras -->
<section id="sec-pythagoras" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Relationship Between Similar Triangles and the Pythagorean Theorem</h2>
  <p>Scaling a right triangle keeps its Pythagorean balance intact.</p>
  <p>Both similar right triangles satisfy <span set:html={rich('[[a^2+b^2=c^2]]')} />. If the original triangle has sides <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />, the scaled triangle has sides <span set:html={rich('[[ka]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[kb]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[kc]]')} />, and the theorem still holds.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[(ka)^2+(kb)^2=(kc)^2]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Expanding gives <span set:html={rich('[[k^2a^2+k^2b^2=k^2c^2]]')} />, and factoring out <span set:html={rich('[[k^2]]')} /> leaves <span set:html={rich('[[k^2(a^2+b^2)=k^2c^2]]')} />. Since <span set:html={rich('[[a^2+b^2=c^2]]')} />, both sides are equal, so similarity preserves the right-angle relationship at any scale.</p>
  <p>Scaling by any positive factor keeps the right angle a right angle, so the scaled triangle is a right triangle too. The angles never change, only the measurements do.</p>
  <p>Check with 3-4-5 and 6-8-10: <span set:html={rich('[[3^2+4^2=9+16=25=5^2]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[6^2+8^2=36+64=100=10^2]]')} />. Both satisfy the theorem, as they must. This is a useful cross-check: if a scaled set of sides fails the theorem, the sides were not scaled by a single factor. For a full treatment of the theorem itself, see the <a href="/pythagorean-theorem-calculator/">Pythagorean theorem calculator</a>.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 15: sec-perimeter-area -->
<section id="sec-perimeter-area" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Perimeter and Area Relationships in Similar Triangles</h2>
  <p>Perimeter scales by k, but area scales by k squared.</p>
  <p>Perimeter is a length, so it changes by the same linear factor as the sides. Area covers two dimensions, so it changes by the square of the scale factor.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{\\frac{P_2}{P_1}=k}]]]')} /></p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\boxed{\\frac{A_2}{A_1}=k^2}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Take the 3-4-5 triangle: its perimeter is <span set:html={rich('[[3+4+5=12]]')} /> and its area is <span set:html={rich('[[(3\\times4)/2=6]]')} />. With <span set:html={rich('[[k=2]]')} /> the sides become 6-8-10, the perimeter becomes <span set:html={rich('[[6+8+10=24]]')} />, and the area becomes <span set:html={rich('[[(6\\times8)/2=24]]')} />. The perimeter doubles, but the area becomes four times as large.</p>
  <p>Doubling the sides never merely doubles the area: at <span set:html={rich('[[k=3]]')} /> the area is nine times as large, and at <span set:html={rich('[[k=0.5]]')} /> it is one-quarter as large. At <span set:html={rich('[[k=1.5]]')} /> the perimeter grows by half again, while the area grows by <span set:html={rich('[[1.5^2=2.25]]')} /> times. These are educational relationships for understanding scaling; the calculator itself has no area or perimeter mode.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('similar-triangles-perimeter-area.svg')} alt="3-4-5 triangle with perimeter 12 and area 6 beside a 6-8-10 triangle with perimeter 24 and area 24" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Doubling the sides doubles the perimeter but quadruples the area.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 16: sec-similar-vs-congruent -->
<section id="sec-similar-vs-congruent" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Difference Between Similar and Congruent Right Triangles</h2>
  <p>Congruent triangles are similar, but similar triangles need not be congruent.</p>
  <p>Similarity and congruence both require equal corresponding angles and the same shape. The difference is size: congruent triangles have equal corresponding sides, while similar triangles only need proportional sides. Every congruent pair is similar with <span set:html={rich('[[k=1]]')} />, but a similar pair with <span set:html={rich('[[k=2]]')} /> is not congruent.</p>
  <p>The test is simple: if the scale factor is <span set:html={rich('[[1]]')} />, the triangles are both similar and congruent. If it is any other positive value, they are similar only. Confusing the two terms is a common exam error, so check the side lengths before claiming congruence.</p>
