</section>

<!-- Section 17: sec-applications -->
<section id="sec-applications" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Real-Life Applications of Similar Right Triangles</h2>
  <p>Similarity turns one known measurement into many unknown ones.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Shadows: a pole of known height and its shadow form a right triangle similar to the one formed by a building and its shadow under the same sun angle, so the building height follows from the ratio of the shadows.</li>
    <li>Blueprints: a scale drawing represents a building at a fixed ratio, so a wall drawn at 6 cm on a 1:50 plan stands for a real wall of 300 cm, found by multiplying by the scale factor.</li>
    <li>Construction: scaled designs and technical drawings keep every length proportional, so a detail drawn at half scale can be read back to full size by dividing by 0.5.</li>
    <li>Model building: a model 18 cm long at 1:24 scale stands for a real car 432 cm long, since every dimension is multiplied by the same scale factor.</li>
    <li>Surveying: triangle proportions estimate heights and distances that cannot be measured directly, such as the width of a river, by comparing a small measured triangle with a larger similar one.</li>
    <li>Classroom geometry: students use similar triangles to find missing lengths in homework and exams, checking each ratio before trusting the answer.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>These are hypothetical examples for learning the method. The calculator works with the numbers you enter; it does not measure real objects or take readings from the field.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 18: sec-mistakes -->
<section id="sec-mistakes" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Common Mistakes When Solving Similar Right Triangle Problems</h2>
  <p>Fifteen mistakes, each with the fix beside it.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Matching the wrong corresponding sides: a leg divided by a hypotenuse gives a meaningless ratio, so match shorter leg to shorter leg, longer leg to longer leg, and hypotenuse to hypotenuse.</li>
    <li>Dividing in the wrong direction when finding k: the scale factor is Similar divided by Original, so <span set:html={rich('[[18/6=3]]')} />, not <span set:html={rich('[[6/18]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Multiplying when the original side is required: recovering the original side needs division by <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[15/3=5]]')} />, not <span set:html={rich('[[15\\times3]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Dividing when finding a larger similar side: moving to a larger triangle needs multiplication, so <span set:html={rich('[[8\\times2.5=20]]')} />, not <span set:html={rich('[[8/2.5]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Using different scale factors for different side pairs: one similar triangle has one <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} />, so every ratio <span set:html={rich('[[a\'/a]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[b\'/b]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[c\'/c]]')} /> must agree.</li>
    <li>Assuming every right triangle is similar: two right triangles with different acute angles are not similar, since their shapes differ.</li>
    <li>Confusing similarity with congruence: similar triangles may differ in size, while congruent triangles must have equal sides and <span set:html={rich('[[k=1]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Forgetting that the scale factor must be positive: a negative <span set:html={rich('[[k]]')} /> has no meaning as a length change, and the calculator rejects it.</li>
    <li>Using zero as a scale factor: <span set:html={rich('[[k=0]]')} /> would collapse every side to zero, which is not a triangle.</li>
    <li>Mixing measurement units: compare centimetres with centimetres, not centimetres with metres, or the ratio is wrong by a factor of 100.</li>
    <li>Assuming a larger triangle has different angles: scaling changes side lengths only, so the acute angles stay exactly the same.</li>
    <li>Confusing the hypotenuse with a leg: the hypotenuse is the longest side, opposite the right angle, and it corresponds to the other hypotenuse.</li>
    <li>Using an inconsistent vertex correspondence: label matching vertices in the same order, or the side pairings will be wrong.</li>
    <li>Assuming doubling side lengths doubles the area: doubling the sides multiplies the area by four, since area scales by <span set:html={rich('[[k^2]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Comparing non-corresponding sides: a ratio is only a scale factor when both sides occupy the same position in their triangles.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

